\abstract{  \OnePageChapter	% one page only ??
  Retargetable compilation has been an active research area in the last few years. In this work, we present a technique that can automatically retarget the code generation phase of a compiler. 
This approach is a solution to the m$\star$n problem. An Automatic Code Generator produces a code generator using the machine description of a target machine. The code generator produced, takes the intermediate code in the form of small instruction trees as input, and produces a low level intermediate representation called Register Transfer Language (RTL). Generated RTL code is a machine independent representation of the input program. Register allocation is done on this RTL code and the final assembly code is outputted. In this work, we are mainly concentrating on 32 bit RISC machines. Code generator for MIPS and SPARC has been produced successfully. \\
The machine description used is similar to that of GNU C Compiler (GCC).  The machine description is organized in three files according to the nature of the architecture information specified. The machine description includes a machine.md file, machine.h file and a machine.c file. 
The machine. md file contains a sequence of instruction patterns for each small operation which occurs as part of the intermediate code. Each instruction pattern has a standard pattern name specifing the operation done by that instruction pattern, an RTL template for RTL generation, and an ouput template specifing the assembly code to be produced for that operation. 
The file machine. h file contains macro definitions which specifies other machine related informations such as storage layout, source language data type layout, register usage and stack layout. The file machine.c contains some machine related functions. 
	}


